<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div><p>At the opening of the <em>Enquiry</em>, Hume distinguishes two types of philosophy. What does he see as the primary weakness of the more popular, literary type?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">It relies too heavily on logical argument and ignores lived experience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">It is entertaining but fails to investigate the principles underlying human thought and conduct.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">It is inaccessible to most readers because of its technical vocabulary.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">It focuses on metaphysics rather than ethics and politics.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div><p>Hume's stated ambition in the <em>Enquiry</em> is modeled on Newton's achievement in physics. What exactly does Hume hope to do for the mind that Newton did for bodies?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Prove mathematically that the mind exists independently of the body.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Discover a small set of principles that govern the operations of the understanding, just as Newton discovered laws governing motion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Demonstrate that mental events obey the same physical laws as matter.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Establish that God is necessary to explain the regularity of thought, just as Newton's laws require a divine creator.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div><p>According to Hume, what is the primary difference between impressions and ideas?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Impressions are caused by external objects; ideas are caused by internal reflection.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Impressions are forceful and vivid; ideas are fainter copies of impressions.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Impressions are simple; ideas can be complex.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Impressions are true; ideas can be false.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div><p>Hume's Copy Principle states that every simple idea is a copy of a corresponding simple impression. What is the philosophical significance of this principle?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">It proves that the external world exists independently of the mind.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">It provides a test for whether a philosophical concept is meaningful: if no impression corresponds to it, the concept is suspect.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">It shows that complex ideas are more reliable than simple ones.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">It establishes that all human beings share the same fundamental experiences.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div><p>Hume acknowledges that the imagination can form ideas of things never directly experienced — for example, a golden mountain. How does he account for this without abandoning the Copy Principle?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">He argues that we must have experienced golden mountains in a previous life.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">He allows an exception to the Copy Principle for imaginative ideas.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">Complex ideas like "golden mountain" are assembled from simple ideas (gold, mountain) each of which does copy an original impression.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">He denies that we truly have an idea of a golden mountain — we merely have the words for it.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div><p>Hume identifies three principles by which ideas become associated in the mind. Which of the following correctly identifies all three?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">Resemblance, contiguity, and cause and effect</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">Resemblance, contrast, and temporal sequence</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">Similarity, memory, and emotional association</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">Identity, succession, and causation</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div><p>When Hume describes the three laws of association, he is careful to say that these are laws governing the way the mind connects ideas — not laws about how the external world works. Why does this distinction matter?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">It means that Hume is only interested in psychology, not philosophy.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">It leaves open the question of whether the connections the mind forms — especially causal connections — reflect real features of the world, which Hume will examine in Section 4.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">It shows that the laws of association do not apply to emotional impressions, only cognitive ones.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">It establishes that the mind is independent of the body and therefore not subject to physical laws.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div><p>Hume's Fork divides all objects of human reason into "relations of ideas" and "matters of fact." Which of the following statements is true of <em>relations of ideas</em>?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">They are discovered through careful observation and can be revised by experience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Their contraries are conceivable but are known to be false through experiment.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">They are knowable through reason alone, and their contraries are inconceivable or self-contradictory.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">They include all claims about God, the soul, and the external world.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div><p>Hume says that all reasoning about matters of fact is founded on the relation of cause and effect. Why does he then go on to argue that this relation itself cannot be established by reason?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Because causal knowledge requires mathematical proof, and mathematics cannot be applied to empirical events.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Because effects are always totally different from their causes — there is no necessary connection between them that reason can detect without prior experience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Because the senses are unreliable, and causal inference requires sensory data.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Because Hume believes causation is an illusion and therefore not knowable by any means.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div><p>Hume's problem of induction shows that we cannot rationally justify the belief that the future will resemble the past. What makes any attempted justification circular?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Because all justifications of induction rely on deductive reasoning, which is inapplicable to matters of fact.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Because any experiential justification ("it has worked before, so it will work again") is itself an inductive inference — it assumes the very principle it is meant to justify.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Because the future has not yet happened and therefore cannot provide evidence for any conclusion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Because induction relies on the senses, which Hume shows are fundamentally unreliable.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q11c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">11</span> <div><p>Hume applies the Copy Principle to the idea of "necessary connection" in causal relations. What does his investigation reveal?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11a" id="q11a">
        <label for="q11a">We derive the idea of necessary connection from a direct impression of causal power when we observe events.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11b" id="q11b">
        <label for="q11b">The idea of necessary connection has no corresponding impression and is therefore meaningless.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11c" id="q11c">
        <label for="q11c">Necessary connection is derived from an internal impression — the mind's habituated expectation that arises after repeated observations of constant conjunction.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11d" id="q11d">
        <label for="q11d">God implants the idea of necessary connection directly into the mind, which is why we feel such certainty about causal claims.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q12c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">12</span> <div><p>When we observe one billiard ball striking another and the second ball moving, Hume says all we actually observe is:</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12a" id="q12a">
        <label for="q12a">The causal power of the first ball being transmitted to the second.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12b" id="q12b">
        <label for="q12b">The necessary connection between the motion of the two balls.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12c" id="q12c">
        <label for="q12c">The constant conjunction of two events: the motion of the first ball followed by the motion of the second.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12d" id="q12d">
        <label for="q12d">A mathematical relationship between the forces involved.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q13c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">13</span> <div><p>In Section 5, Hume offers his "sceptical solution" to the problem of induction. What is the solution?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13a" id="q13a">
        <label for="q13a">Reason can justify induction if we use it carefully enough, and the problem arises only from careless application.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13b" id="q13b">
        <label for="q13b">God guarantees the reliability of induction, just as Descartes argued God guarantees the reliability of reason.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13c" id="q13c">
        <label for="q13c">Our inductive inferences are not justified by reason but by custom and habit — a natural, non-rational tendency of the mind that we cannot avoid and that serves us well enough.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13d" id="q13d">
        <label for="q13d">Induction is actually a form of deductive reasoning and is therefore as certain as mathematics.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q14c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">14</span> <div><p>According to Hume, what distinguishes a <em>belief</em> from a merely entertained idea?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14a" id="q14a">
        <label for="q14a">A belief has been verified by reason; a merely entertained idea has not.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14b" id="q14b">
        <label for="q14b">A belief corresponds to a real state of the external world; an idea may not.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14c" id="q14c">
        <label for="q14c">A belief is an idea held with a particular vivacity and force, produced by custom and experience rather than by rational argument.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14d" id="q14d">
        <label for="q14d">A belief is innate; ideas are acquired through experience.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q15b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">15</span> <div><p>In Meditation III, Descartes argued that the idea of an infinitely perfect God must have an equally perfect cause, and that this cause must be God himself. How does Hume's analysis of causation and the Copy Principle challenge this argument?</p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15a" id="q15a">
        <label for="q15a">Hume agrees with the argument's logic but denies that we have an idea of perfection, since no impression of perfection exists.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15b" id="q15b">
        <label for="q15b">Hume shows that the causal principle Descartes relies on — that every effect requires a cause at least as great — is not a relation of ideas (it is conceivable that it be false) and is not established by experience, so it cannot bear the weight Descartes places on it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15c" id="q15c">
        <label for="q15c">Hume accepts that we have an idea of God but denies that this idea has a cause, since ideas need not have causes.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15d" id="q15d">
        <label for="q15d">Hume's critique is irrelevant to Descartes because Descartes was reasoning about necessary truths, not matters of fact.</label>
      </li>
    </ul><button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  
  
</div>
